Title: --legacy-sandbox, and make a sandboxed launch failure say why
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo), after Supermium
       (https://github.com/win32ss/supermium)

Part of this is adapted from Supermium's sandbox patch, which reached the
problem first. Most of that patch is already in this tree --
patches/chromium/sandbox/0202 and 0319 restore the per-version ntdll
service-interception machinery it depends on, and the resolver thunks,
win_utils helpers and Vista token gates are all present -- so what is here is
the part that was missing, plus the diagnostics that made the remaining failure
legible.

--- The switch ---------------------------------------------------------------

Supermium drops the utility-process sandbox for every Windows below 10
outright, and offers --legacy-sandbox for the renderer and GPU. This keeps the
sandbox on by default everywhere and puts all three behind the flag, because XP,
7, 8.1 and 10 are sandboxing correctly here: disabling it for all of them to
insure against a failure we have not seen would be a straight loss of a real
security boundary. No version check is involved; the flag does nothing unless
asked for. It is also narrower than --no-sandbox, which disables the machinery
everywhere including the broker.

Three places have to agree, which is why this touches more than one file:

  - SandboxTypeFromCommandLine tells a child what it is. This is the part
    Supermium changes.
  - The browser decides whether to sandbox a child from the delegate's sandbox
    type, which is fixed per process type and never reads a command line, so
    IsUnsandboxedProcess has to answer the flag too. Without it the browser
    would build a policy and spawn through the broker while the child believed
    it was unsandboxed.
  - The flag has to reach the child at all, so it joins kNoSandbox in the switch
    lists the renderer, GPU and utility hosts forward. Otherwise a child would
    try to initialize sandbox target services the browser never set up.

Also from Supermium: a 32-bit child on 64-bit XP, Vista or 7 can reach
interception setup before its ntdll is readable, and every ReadProcessMemory
against it fails with ERROR_PARTIAL_COPY until it is, taking the thunk setup
with it. Waiting for the first read to land fixes it. Bounded here, unlike the
original, which spins without a limit -- this runs on the browser's
process-launcher thread, so an unbounded wait would stall every later child
launch rather than failing one, and giving up merely restores the behaviour
without the loop.

--- The diagnostics ----------------------------------------------------------

Two paths report a sandboxed child that never started, and in a release build
neither says anything:

  - GeneratePolicyForSandboxedProcess failing is silent upstream. Not a DLOG,
    nothing. This is the interesting one for an old OS, because it is where a
    policy the running Windows cannot express would be rejected.
  - FinishStartSandboxedProcess logs with DLOG and DPLOG, which compile to
    nothing when NDEBUG is defined. An official build with
    dcheck_always_on = false drops the one message naming the ResultCode, and
    --enable-logging --v=1 cannot bring back a call that is not in the binary.

The symptom is a child that simply never appears, indistinguishable from that
process type not being used. Both now log at ERROR with the delegate's sandbox
tag, so the message names the process type and the ResultCode from
sandbox/win/src/sandbox_types.h.

That is not decoration: it is what identified the one platform still failing.
64-bit pre-Win7 Windows reports

  Failed to launch sandboxed process "gpu!kGpu": ResultCode 7, last error 0

which is SBOX_ERROR_NO_HANDLE from InterceptionManager::PatchClientFunctions.
On <= Vista that calls Wow64::WaitForNtdll, which returns true immediately
unless the child is a WoW64 process, and otherwise launches wow_helper.exe from
beside the running exe. Electron never packaged wow_helper.exe -- it is a
Chromium sandbox artifact, named nowhere in electron/ -- so on a 64-bit XP or
Vista host the CreateProcess failed and every child launch failed with it.
32-bit hosts never enter that path, which is why 32-bit XP sandboxed correctly
and a 64-bit Vista did not. Win7 and later skip the block entirely.

So this was never a Vista problem, or a flaky one: it is a missing file on a
64-bit host, deterministically. The last hunk ships it. GN already copies it
into the output directory for an x86 build (//sandbox/win:copy_wow_helper,
reached through //sandbox/win:sandbox's deps), but dist_zip packs runtime deps,
and a runtime-deps walk does not follow a plain deps edge into a copy target --
so naming it as a data_dep of the zip is what puts it in the package. Confirmed
on a 64-bit Vista: with the file beside electron.exe, every child launches
sandboxed.

